reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:252	rule	attrs=concrete	decision=FIXED_SUPPLIED_BASELINE	rule roundF(F:Float) => #if (F -Float floorFloat(F)) ==Float 0.5 #then (#if Float2Int(floorFloat(F)) %Int 2 ==Int 0 #then Float2Int(floorFloat(F)) #else Float2Int(ceilFloat(F)) #fi) #else Float2Int(floorFloat(F +Float 0.5)) #fi [concrete] // round(F, N): CPython rounds the EXACT binary value of F at the Nth decimal // place, half-to-even, then converts to the nearest double. The old code did // Int2Float(roundF(F *Float 10^N)) / 10^N — the float multiply F *Float 10^N // misrounds the half-way decision (witness: a Heron triangle area that must // round to 2.69 came out 2.68). Exact version, via arbitrary-precision integers: //     F = numer / 2^k      numer = Float2Int(F * 2^(52-e)),  k = 52 - exponentFloat(F) //     q = round-half-even( numer * 10^N , 2^k )        (exact big-integer round) //     result = nearest double to q / 10^N //   IEEE division is correctly rounded, so (double)q /Float (double)10^N equals //   the nearest double to q/10^N whenever q and 10^N are exactly representable. // Guarded to finite F with |F| < 1e12 and |F|*10^N < 2^52 (keeps numer and q // exactly representable) and a non-subnormal exponent (avoids 2^k overflowing a // double); the exotic tail keeps the old path, so nothing regresses. // Opaque for kprove, [concrete] for krun.
